<thoughtml>
  <investigation subject="collapsed test loaf">
    <observations>
      <observation id="o1">The yeast was fresh.</observation>
      <observation id="o2">The oven reached its target temperature.</observation>
      <observation id="o3">The dough received twice the intended amount of water.</observation>
      <observation id="o4">The proofing time was slightly shorter than intended.</observation>
    </observations>
    <hypotheses>
      <hypothesis id="h1">Excessive hydration weakened the dough structure and caused the loaf to collapse.</hypothesis>
      <hypothesis id="h2">Insufficient proofing contributed to or caused the collapse.</hypothesis>
      <hypothesis id="h3">Inactive yeast caused the collapse.</hypothesis>
      <hypothesis id="h4">Incorrect oven temperature caused the collapse.</hypothesis>
    </hypotheses>
    <evidence>
      <support hypothesis="h1" observation="o3" strength="strong">The water quantity was a major deviation from the intended formula and could leave the dough unable to retain its shape.</support>
      <support hypothesis="h2" observation="o4" strength="weak">The shortened proofing time is a competing explanation and may have contributed to inadequate structure or rise.</support>
      <against hypothesis="h3" observation="o1" strength="moderate">Fresh yeast makes yeast failure less likely, although freshness alone does not prove normal activity.</against>
      <against hypothesis="h4" observation="o2" strength="moderate">Reaching the target temperature makes insufficient oven heat less likely, although it does not rule out temperature variation during baking.</against>
    </evidence>
    <conclusion status="provisional" confidence="moderate">
      Excessive hydration is presently the leading explanation for the collapse because the dough received twice the intended water. Slightly short proofing remains a plausible contributing factor, but the available evidence does not establish it as the primary cause. A controlled repeat using the intended water quantity and proofing time would test this conclusion.
    </conclusion>
  </investigation>
</thoughtml>